<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Accuracy (%) after Selective Self<span id="S5.T2.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>Reflection on the <math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\%</annotation></semantics></math> least<span id="S5.T2.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>confident samples.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM Cascading.</span>
Instead of reflecting, we can <em id="S5.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">escalate</em> low<span id="S5.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>confidence queries to a more capable (larger but slower) model.
In our setting, low-confidence cases are routed to Llama3<span id="S5.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>70B-Instruct with the same prompt, while the rest are handled by the original Llama3<span id="S5.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>8B-Instruct.
As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5 Applications ‣ All Roads Lead to Rome: Graph-Based Confidence Estimation for Large Language Model Reasoning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, cascading just the least-confident 15% of queries yields accuracy improvements of around <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+2</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+5</annotation></semantics></math> points.</p>
</div>
